NAME
    cp - copy files and directory trees

SYNOPSIS
    cp src dst
    cp glob dir
    cp -r srcdir dstdir

DESCRIPTION
    Copies the file src to dst. When dst is an existing directory the
    copy goes into it under the source's name (cp A.TXT /T makes
    /T/A.TXT). A file of the target name is replaced. The load and exec
    addresses are kept, so a copied program runs from its new place.
    cp is quiet when it works. cp is /BIN/CP.

    A glob source (* or ? in the last component, case folded) copies
    every matching file into dst, which must be an existing directory.

    With -r the directory srcdir and everything below it is copied as
    dstdir, which is made if it does not exist; files are replaced,
    directories that exist are reused. Copying a directory into itself
    (or below itself) is refused.

    Messages: "cp: not found: X", "cp: a directory (use -r): X",
    "cp: not a file: X", "cp: cannot open X", "cp: cannot create X",
    "cp: cannot make X", "cp: write error: X", "cp: no match: X",
    "cp: the target of a glob must be a directory",
    "cp: cannot copy a directory into itself".

OPTIONS
    -r    copy a directory tree (-R works too)

LIMITS
    Inside a > or >> redirect or a pipe (stdout going to a file) the
    OS refuses a second file open for writing: cp then says "cp:
    cannot create X" and copies nothing (see shell). cp < F works.
    A glob copies at most 24 files. -r descends 8 levels: a deeper
    directory is made but not filled, and cp says "cp: deeper than 8
    levels, not all copied". src and dst are cut at 63 characters.

EXAMPLES
    cp /FRUIT.TXT /F.TXT      copy one file
    cp /FRUIT.TXT /T          into the directory /T: /T/FRUIT.TXT
    cp /*.TXT /T              every .TXT file of / into /T
    cp /BIN/HELLO /HI         a program: `hi` typed in / runs it
    cp -r /MAN /MANBAK        a whole directory

NOTES
    Removed from the P8X version: the /d1 mount. Changed: load and exec
    are copied (the P8X wrote 0/0), a single file can go into a
    directory, -r has no limit of 24 entries per level, and cp no
    longer prints the byte count (the first Y1/OS cp did).
    Ported from P8X os/commands/cp.c 2026-09-23.

